e7 wire log v1
device   GS Music e7, serial 361, USB, driven from Node over raw CoreMIDI
input    GS-e7 Analog Synthesizer
output   GS-e7 Analog Synthesizer
date     2026-08-24
session  One Read Memory answer at a time, logged per driver packet, showing the ten bytes the instrument withholds

# Every line here is one driver packet, not one frame: this was taken over raw CoreMIDI
# with no SysEx reassembly, which is the only way the thing below is visible at all.
# 
# A Read Memory answer is 34 bytes. Twenty-four of them arrive 5-10ms after the command,
# paced at MIDI's own 31250 baud. The last ten, the F7 among them, are not sent: the
# instrument's MIDI output retains a ten-byte residue and ships only what is queued
# behind it. Waiting never releases it — five seconds changes nothing — and the next
# command's answer is what pushes it out, ahead of itself.
# 
# So a client that reassembles F0..F7 cannot complete a frame until the send after the
# one it answers, which is why every answer on this path appears to trail its command by
# one. It is the instrument's, not the transport's: Web MIDI in Chrome shows it on the
# same machine and the same cable, and another USB-MIDI device on the same driver does
# not.
